{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788507515-uLQs5hsSSvWMxx0JuCev","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now docs/usage.rst — two examples. First one:\n\n```\n    >>> data = jwt.decode(\n    ...     token,\n    ...     signing_key.key,\n    ...     algorithms=[\"RS256\"],\n    ...     audience=\"https://expenses-api\",\n    ...     options={\"verify_exp\": False},\n    ... )\n```\n→\n```\n    >>> data = jwt.decode(\n    ...     token,\n    ...     signing_key,\n    ...     audience=\"https://expenses-api\",\n    ...     options={\"verify_exp\": False},\n    ... )\n```\n\nSecond:\n```\n    data = jwt.decode_complete(\n        id_token,\n        key=signing_key.key,\n        algorithms=signing_algos,\n        audience=client_id,\n    )\n```\n→\n```\n    data = jwt.decode_complete(\n        id_token,\n        key=signing_key,\n        audience=client_id,\n    )\n```\n\nWait — the second example: after removing `algorithms=signing_algos`, is `signing_algos` still used elsewhere in that example? Let me check the surrounding context. Earlier in the example there's probably `signing_algos = ...` or the alg detection. Let me look at lines 336-380 of usage.rst to see whether `signing_algos` is used later (e.g. to check the algorithm isn't custom). The upstream diff only removed those lines, so presumably `signing_algos` is only used there or is used in the remaining code... Let me check to be safe.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Together","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee8da7a8-0bbe-4466-be9c-5dd0aa2755a7","uuid":"13553078-c776-47a7-90f4-a57ecba2321e