t algorithms.\n\nDoes it break existing tests? Let me check tests/test_api_jwt.py for tests that expect DecodeError when algorithms missing:\n\n- `test_decode_raises_exception_if_algorithms_missing` — uses a string key presumably → still raises. \n\nLet me check for any test that constructs PyJWK and calls jwt.decode without algorithms expecting failure — very unlikely (feature didn't exist).\n\nAlso does relaxing change behavior for `PyJWT().decode_complete(...)` when key is PyJWK and algorithms None → now delegates to api_jws which derives algorithms → works. \n\nAnd \"mismatched algorithm\" path: `jwt.decode(token, jwk_with_HS512)` where token alg=HS256 → _verify_signature: algorithms=[HS512], alg=HS256 not in list → InvalidAlgorithmError. Good.\n\nOne more consideration: `options[\"verify_signature\"] = False` case at api_jwt level: with verify_signature False and no algorithms, previously passed the guard (falsy), fine — unchanged.\n\nNow, what about `api_jwt.decode_complete` passing `algorithms` down: unchanged, api_jws handles the PyJWK derivation.\n\nI'm confident this extension is correct and needed to fulfill the user's stated requirements. Also, it makes the updated docs example (`jwt.decode(token, signing_key, audience=...)` without algorithms) actually work — currently, with PR #886 alone, that docs example would crash! That's strong evidence the api_jwt-level relaxation is genuinely needed: upstream's own 2.9.0 docs (generated from PR #886's doc change) didn't work until the later refactor. The user asked for docs examples that \"just show passing signing_key directly\" AND for it to work consistently across jwt.decode() and decode_complete(). So the api_jwt relaxation is required for the user's spec to hold.\n\nLet me implement it: modify api_jwt.py decode_complete guard + runtime import.\n\napi_jwt.py currently:\n```python\nfrom . import api_jws\nfrom .exceptions import (...)\nfrom .warnings import RemovedInPyjwt3Warning\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .algorithms import AllowedPrivateKeys, AllowedPublicKeys\n    from .api_jwk import PyJWK\n```\n\nI'll add `from .api_jwk import PyJWK` at runtime. Then the TYPE_CHECKING import of PyJWK becomes redundant — remove it (keep the annotation working via the runtime import). Actually with `from __future__ import annotations`, annotations are strings, so runtime import isn't needed for annotations, but isinstance needs it. I'll move PyJWK import to runtime and keep TYPE_CHECKING block for algorithms' Allowed* types.\n\nThen:\n```python\n        if options[\"verify_signature\"] and not algorithms and not isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n            raise DecodeError(...)\n```\n\nLet me apply.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Z.AI","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee8da7a8-0bbe-4466-be9c-5dd0aa2755a7","uuid":"93cdfa8d-abec-441b-936f-60d3e7acab60","timestamp":"2026-09-04T07:45:00.543Z","request_id":"gen-1788507866-QAUPF8kZZYFHZ6yHEPbr"}
