{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788508108-6ttHhi22UXbLuy7CuLoC","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All done — 21 JWK tests pass, full suite is green (264 passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed).\n\n## What I implemented\n\n**1. `PyJWK.algorithm_name`** (`jwt/api_jwk.py:55`) — the JWK now stores its resolved algorithm name, whether it came from the `alg` field, was passed explicitly, or was inferred from `kty`/`crv`. The lookup was also tightened from `self._algorithms.get(algorithm)` + falsy check to an explicit membership test, so `algorithm_name` is always available before the algorithm object is resolved.\n\n**2. `PyJWK` accepted as `key`** in `decode`/`decode_complete` on both `PyJWS` and `PyJWT` (`jwt/api_jws.py`, `jwt/api_jwt.py`), with `PyJWK` added to the signatures. When the key is a `PyJWK`, `_verify_signature` uses `key.Algorithm` and the already-prepared `key.key` directly instead of re-looking-up and re-preparing the key from the header's `alg`.\n\n**3. Auto-derived algorithms** — the \"algorithms argument required\" guard now exempts `PyJWK` keys, and `_verify_signature` defaults `algorithms` to `[key.algorithm_name]` when none is given. So `jwt.decode(token, jwk)` verifies with the JWK's own algorithm; a token signed with a different `alg` raises `InvalidAlgorithmError` (this is the security property — the algorithm whitelist is pinned to the JWK).\n\nAlso updated the two JWKS examples in `docs/usage.rst` to pass `signing_key` directly (and dropped the now-unused `signing_algos` variable), added the `algorithm_name` assertions and three JWK decode tests from the upstream change, and added CHANGELOG entries.\n\n## One deliberate addition beyond upstream\n\nI implemented this against upstream PR #886 (released in 2.9.0) as the reference. One important difference: upstream 2.9.0 left `api_jwt.PyJWT.decode_complete` with its own stricter guard, so module-level `jwt.decode(token, jwk)` *without* `algorithms` still raised `DecodeError` there — I confirmed this against the 2.9.0 tag, and their own docs example would have crashed. Since your requirement is that this works consistently for both `jwt.decode()` and `decode_complete()`, I relaxed that guard the same way (`jwt/api_jwt.py:147`), with a runtime `PyJWK` import (no circular-import issue — verified importing in all orders). Upstream master has since converged on the same design: the guard lives only in `api_jws`, exempting `PyJWK`.\n\nVerified behaviors: decode with/without `algorithms`, `decode_complete` on both API classes, mismatched JWK algorithm → `InvalidAlgorithmError`, tampered signature → `InvalidSignatureError`, and string keys without `algorithms` still raising `DecodeError`.","citations":[]}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Z.AI","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee8da7a8-0bbe-4466-be9c-5dd0aa2755a7","uuid":"00bc2d65-a250-4a94-bc96-62bb443df119","timestamp":"2026-09-04T07:48:49.643Z","request_id":"gen-1788508108-6ttHhi22UXbLuy7CuLoC"}
